#Bell and Drum Towers

ICON₁ Guan Gong (關公 / 伽藍神王 Sangharama Deva) (Drum Tower, left of Mahavira Hall)
POSITION    : peripheral, elevated, recessed (within niche)
SCALE       : singular (altar scope);
              equal (compound scope, relative to Bell Tower)
ORIENTATION : front-facing (outward from niche);
              inward-facing (tower as unit, toward Mahavira Hall)
FUNCTION    : protective, apotropaic, administrative

Guan Gong — the deified form of the historical general Guan Yu (關羽, died 220 CE) — appears here in his full iconic form: dark reddish-brown face, green ceremonial robes, holding what appears to be a scripture or a tablet. His presence in a Chinese Buddhist monastery requires an ontological clarification that the grammar must encode. In the Chinese Buddhist institutional tradition, Guan Yu is identified as Garan Shenwang (伽藍神王, Sangharama Deva) — the divine protector of the monastic compound (saṃghārāma). This identification is a syncretic integration: the folk and Taoist deity of loyalty, righteousness, and martial power is substituted into the Buddhist protective role, his attributes of absolute fidelity and indomitable strength mapped onto the function of monastery guardianship. 

The substitutes relation encodes this:
[GUAN_GONG  substitutes  SANGHARAMA_DEVA]
His administrative function is worth specifying: as Sangharama Deva, Guan Gong holds an administrative office within the Buddhist institutional hierarchy — he governs the monastery's spatial and spiritual perimeter, maintaining the integrity of the monastic boundary against disruption from malevolent forces, unruly spirits, and worldly intrusions.

ICON₂ Kṣitigarbha Bodhisattva (地藏菩薩) (Bell Tower, right of Mahavira Hall)
POSITION    : peripheral, elevated, recessed (within niche)
SCALE       : singular (altar scope);
              equal (compound scope, relative to Drum Tower)
ORIENTATION : front-facing (outward from niche);
              inward-facing (tower as unit, toward Mahavira Hall)
FUNCTION    : soteriological, intercessory

Kṣitigarbha is depicted in his canonical Chinese Buddhist form: a monk's figure holding a khakkhara (錫杖, the ringed staff that opens the gates of hell) in the right hand and a cintāmaṇi (如意寶珠, wish-fulfilling jewel) in the left. His vow — famously articulated in the Kṣitigarbha Sūtra — is to remain in the world until every being in hell has been liberated before he himself accepts Buddhahood. His soteriological function is therefore specifically oriented toward the most extreme conditions of suffering: he reaches into the lowest registers of existence to retrieve those whom other salvific programmes cannot access. His intercessory function is inseparable from this — he mediates between the living and the dead, between the human community and the afterlife realms, carrying petitions downward and liberation upward.
His placement in the Bell Tower is iconographically resonant in ways discussed below under the History of Religions reading.
